          "  const validate = (time: Time) => {",
          "    setError(error);\n    return error === undefined;\n  };\n\n  // Revalidate when the bounds change and apply a new defaultTime while rendering,\n  // instead of in effects. Both checks also run on mount.\n  const boundsKey = JSON.stringify({ maximumTime, minimumTime });\n  const [validatedBoundsKey, setValidatedBoundsKey] = useState<string>();\n  if (boundsKey !== validatedBoundsKey) {\n    setValidatedBoundsKey(boundsKey);\n    if (minimumTime || maximumTime) {\n      validate(time);\n    }\n  }\n\n  const [appliedDefaultTime, setAppliedDefaultTime] = useState<Time>();\n  if (defaultTime !== appliedDefaultTime) {\n    setAppliedDefaultTime(defaultTime);\n    if (defaultTime) {\n      setTime(defaultTime);\n      validate(defaultTime);\n    }\n  }\n",
          "import { useState } from \"react\";"
        ],
        "finalMessage": "I fixed all six issues in `TimeInput.tsx`, but I couldn't re-run the linter: there's no `node_modules` here, so `oxlint`/`ultracite` aren't installed.\n\n**What was wrong:** the two `useEffect`s only existed to update state when props changed. Their dependency lists left out `validate`, `time`, `minimumTime` and `maximumTime`, and the first one used `JSON.stringify(...)` calls as dependencies. Simply adding the missing values would have changed behavior. With `time` as a dependency, validation would run on every keystroke instead of only on blur. And with the raw bound objects, it would rerun on every parent render.\n\n**What I changed:** I removed both effects and the unused `useEffect` import. In their place I used React's recommended pattern for adjusting state when a prop changes: keep the previous value in state, compare it during render, and update state when it differs.\n- **Bounds:** the code builds `boundsKey = JSON.stringify({ maximumTime, minimumTime })` and stores the last one it handled. When the key changes, it records the new key and, if there are bounds, checks the current `time` against them. Like the old dependency list, this reacts to changes in the bounds' values, not to new object references.\n- **`defaultTime`:** the code stores the last `defaultTime` it applied. When the reference changes, it records it and, if it's set, calls `setTime(defaultTime)` and `validate(defaultTime)`, as before.\n- Both stored values start as `undefined`, so both checks still run on mount, just as the effects did. They run in the original order, so the `defaultTime` check still wins if both fire in the same render.\n- These blocks come after `validate`, because they call it during render.\n\n**One visible difference:** on mount, any validation error now appears in the first render. Before, the component rendered once without the error and then again with it.",
